::: {.doc-subject}

Check if a strongly connected directed graph is aperiodic.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L73164-L73273){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_aperiodic()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is aperiodic](../assets/graphs/is-aperiodic.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: the directed cycle A, B, C, D plus the chord C to A, which closes the 3-cycle A, B, C (accent color) beside the 4-cycle; the cycle lengths 3 and 4 have greatest common divisor 1, so is_aperiodic() returns True. Right: the 4-cycle alone has only cycles of length 4, so its period is 4 and is_aperiodic() returns False; the dashed chord marks the missing edge." width="100%"}

The period of a strongly connected directed graph is the greatest common divisor of the
lengths of all its cycles; the graph is aperiodic when that divisor is 1. A random walk
(Markov chain) on a strongly connected graph converges to a unique stationary
distribution from any start exactly when the graph is aperiodic, which is why PageRank
and MCMC samplers care about it: a bipartite-like cycle structure makes the walk
oscillate instead of settling.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph is aperiodic.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is undirected, empty, or not strongly connected.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

A breadth-first search assigns levels; the period divides `level(u) - level(v) + 1` for
every edge `u -> v`, and the greatest common divisor of those values over all non-tree
edges is the period (Jarvis and Shier, 1996), $O(V + E)$. Matches NetworkX's
`is_aperiodic`.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~is_strongly_connected`) 

:   Check that every node can reach every other.

[](`~strongly_connected_components`) 

:   The strongly connected parts of a graph.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A directed cycle of four has period 4. Adding a chord that closes a cycle of three
makes the period the greatest common divisor of 3 and 4, which is 1:

::: {#74fb0092 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

ring = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_cycle_graph(4)
before = ring.is_aperiodic()
ring.add_edge(3, 1)

before, ring.is_aperiodic()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(False, True)
```
:::
:::


### Money that comes back

`payments()` is a month of payments between 18 accounts at a made-up bank, each directed
from payer to payee. The ring of Ana, Ben and Cruz on its own is periodic: money that
leaves Ana comes back only after a multiple of three payments. The strongly connected
core of eight accounts, which adds the other ring and the mutual pair Dee and Eve, has
cycles of length 2 and 3 and so is aperiodic:

::: {#93a7ebaf .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
payments = ec.payments()

def accounts(*names):
    return payments.subgraph(
        [payments.get_id_from_label(name) for name in names]
    )

ring = accounts("Ana", "Ben", "Cruz")
core = accounts("Ana", "Ben", "Cruz", "Dee", "Eve", "Finn", "Gus", "Hal")

ring.is_aperiodic(), core.is_aperiodic()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(False, True)
```
:::
